* sg13cmos5l closed-loop vref statistical-MC bench
* sg13cmos5l-bandgap -- deck body for the device-variation Monte Carlo campaigns
* (issue #31, T1 item 6). sim/mc-run.sh substitutes this file's three placeholders --
* the supply value, the scratch directory and the statistical-scale block -- and writes
* the deck to sim/build/closed-loop-vref-mc/deck.cir. The placeholder TOKENS are never
* spelled out in these comments: the scale block expands to several lines, and a comment
* line holding the token would expand into a comment followed by live cards.
* Model `.lib` cards, temperature, the supply value and the per-sample
* `.options seed=` card all come from the `klt sim` request.
*
* The fixture is deliberately IDENTICAL to sim/closed-loop-vref-pvt/vref.cir.tmpl:
* same supply source, same vss, same `sub!` tie, same convergence aids, same DUT
* include. That is what makes the zero-spread control reproduce the item-5
* deterministic figure exactly (sim/closed-loop-vref-mc/README.md), so any spread
* this bench reports is attributable to the model sections the request selects and
* to nothing in the testbench.
*
* The DUT is the in-tree migrated netlist design/sg13cmos5l/netlist/bandgap_top.spice
* (decision record 0006); sim/runner-lib.sh's cm5_stage_dut copies it into the scratch
* directory with only its final `.end` card removed.
*
* NO SPICE TITLE LINE (issue #19): `klt sim` `.include`s this deck into a top deck
* of its own, so line 1 here is not in title position. Every line is a comment or a
* card, and no `.control` block appears here -- OSDI objects load through the
* request's options.osdi_preload.

* rshunt/gmin: convergence aids only, far below any current read here (the same
* values the item-5 closed-loop vref bench uses).
.options rshunt=1e9 gmin=1e-9

Vvdd vdd 0 dc @@VDD@@
Vvss vss 0 dc 0
Vsub sub! vss dc 0

.include "@@WORK@@/bandgap_top.spice"

* Statistical-magnitude scale block. EMPTY for every campaign that runs the pinned
* model libraries unchanged; for the known-bad control sim/mc-run.sh generates one
* `.param` per statistical magnitude, read out of the PINNED libraries and multiplied
* by the campaign's `stat_scale`, so the inflated sigmas can never drift from the pin.
* These cards sit after the request's `.lib` cards, and ngspice resolves a `.param`
* to its LAST definition, so they win (measured: sim/closed-loop-vref-mc/README.md
* "The known-bad control"). This is the hand-built equivalent of `klt sim`'s
* `monte_carlo.mismatch_scale`, which covers sky130 and gf180mcu only.
@@STAT_SCALE_BLOCK@@
